    O3  Resources, named operation families, and state
        - resource descriptors own descriptor ID, synchronous key projector, lookup adapter, and
          descriptor-owned freshness/collection policy. P is complete executable input retained by a
          lookup, transaction attempt, or stream subscription; P may contain clients/functions and
          is never identity. Runtime capabilities remain outside P; service dependencies are
          inferred as Effect/Stream R. K is the ordered canonical tuple key(P); resource identity is
          descriptor ID plus K. Project, validate, and canonicalize before ownership, mutation,
          admission, or external work. Methods use complete P for execution and exact K for passive
          reads.

        - Canonical source containers are ordinary dense arrays/plain records, recursively
          defensively copied together with the top tuple. Reject undefined, non-finite numbers,
          bigint, symbols, functions, accessors, classes, unsupported objects, cycles, branded
          secrets, sparse/extra property arrays, hostile or inconsistent reflection. Bounds are
          depth 16, 256 value nodes, and 8192 UTF-8 bytes. Failures identify exact K[index] or
          nested-record paths. KBytes is whitespace-free canonical JSON-like grammar:
          null/booleans/finite numbers/JSON-escaped strings, dense arrays, and records serialized by
          raw UTF-16-sorted keys. Numbers use finite JSON.stringify and normalize -0 to 0; strings
          are not normalized. Records require Object.prototype or null prototype and enumerable own
          data properties without symbols. Arrays are ordinary, dense, and have no extra properties.
          Read data descriptors only; never run getters, coercion, toJSON, or user iteration. Every
          result-changing discriminator belongs in K. There is no second key, custom hash/equality,
          or new freshness default. Tags derive from K; placeholders are passive metadata. Invalid
          inputs publish/mutate nothing and start no work; equal bytes share identity, different
          bytes separate identity. persist defaults false.

        - The public generic family shapes are:
          - ResourceFamily<P, K extends unknown[], A, E> has key(P): K.
            It has getData(K): A | undefined and getState(K): ResourceState<A,E,K>.
          - It has lookup(P, options?: FiniteResourceOptions<A,E>): FiniteOperationPlan.
          - It has subscribe(P, options?: ResourceSubscriptionOptions<A,E>):
            ContinuingOperationPlan.
          - It has refetch(P, options?: FiniteResourceOptions<A,E>): FiniteOperationPlan.
          - It has setData(K, A | ((A | undefined) => A | undefined)): CacheWritePlan.
          - It has cancel(K): CancellationPlan.
          - TransactionFamily<P,K extends unknown[],A,E> has key, getState,
            commit(P, options?: CommitOptions<P,A,E>): TransactionCommitPlan, and cancel(K).
          - StreamFamily<P,K extends unknown[],V,E> has key, getState, and
            subscribe(P, options?: StreamSubscriptionOptions<P,K,V,E>): ContinuingOperationPlan.
          These schematic aliases describe exact family shapes. Standalone support aliases are not
          public exports.

        - ResourceState is a closed union:
          - missing(key), pending(key,generation), ready(key,generation,data), and
            refreshing(key,generation,data).
          - failure(key,generation,error) intersects optional retained data.
          - defect(key,generation,defect) intersects optional retained data.
          - interrupted(key,generation) intersects optional retained data.

        - TransactionState is a closed union:
          - idle(key) and pending(key,generation).
          - success(key,generation,value) and failure(key,generation,error).
          - defect(key,generation,defect) and interrupted(key,generation).
          - unknown(key,generation,reconcileRequired:true).

        - StreamValue is either hasValue:false, latest?:never, emissionCount:0 or
          hasValue:true, latest:V, emissionCount:number.
          StreamState adds idle(key,generation:null) and running/complete/failure/defect/interrupted
          states with that StreamValue.
          Undefined is not A; it means missing getData or updater decline. Counts and generations
          are non-negative safe integers. Resource failures/defects/interruption may retain data;
          refreshing always does. Unknown is distinct from interruption.

        - Passive reads never acquire, start, refresh, mutate, collect, or materialize missing
          entries. Omitted transaction key is []. Accepted verbs are lookup, commit, subscribe;
          accepted action/continuing declarations alone execute operations and retain full P.
          Invalidate and clear resolve exact resource/K, tag, and family targets against one
          pre-mutation snapshot.
          Resolution uses stable descriptor/key order and keeps first-seen identities.
          The batch caps at 256 identities; zero matches are a no-op.
          Invalidate retains data and overlays.
          Invalidate does not replace or cancel generations.
          Clear fences work, cancels the final owner, and removes data and overlays.
          Clear leaves surviving entries missing.
          Conflicting target changes reject.

        - Finite actions admit one inert plan, list, null, or empty list; accepted work is
          lookup/refetch, commit/cancel, cache write, invalidate, clear. Omission/null/empty means
          none. Actions are not inferred from timers, activation, passive reads, memory/store
          revisions, completion, or reconciliation. Finite outcomes map success(A), failure(E),
          defect(), interrupt(); continuing resources map value(A), failure(E), defect(),
          interrupt(). Mappers return one typed event and receive no Exit/Cause/state/lifecycle.
          Occurrences admit once; reconciliation does not restart them. Continuing resources are
          activities; memory-derived plans are onMemory. Declarations own subscriptions; machine
          code does not manually unsubscribe for correctness. after targets an explicit refresh
          event; it is not polling.

        - Transaction family exposes passive key/getState, finite commit, and actor-owned cancel.
          Missing key is []. commit(P,options?) is inert until an accepted event action. Actor-local
          descriptor+K concurrency is exactly reject | cancel | allow | serialize; retry is a new
          commit from a new event. Outcomes distinguish success/failure/defect/interruption. Writes
          are explicit setData plans; result values never become canonical data implicitly. No
          completion-side invalidates/clears options, parent
          machine/memory/event/selector/route/preview capture, or retry helper.

        - Stream family exposes passive key/getState and continuing subscribe; it has no actor
          cancel and no resource entry. One live declaration with the same actor+descriptor+K
          rejects before replacement/release; other actors are independent. Equal key retains
          generation and original P; changed key releases once and starts another.
          Exit/replacement/disposal/false/null finalizes once; late emissions are fenced by
          slot/key/generation; values coalesce to latest projection. Outcomes are value(V),
          complete(), failure(E), defect(), interrupt(); release maps no outcome. Hydration
          rematerializes active declarations from live P without replaying emissions. Terminal
          streams do not restart; missing P fails closed rather than reconstructing from K.
          Invalidate/clear operate on exact [O.resource,K], reachable tags, and admitted resource
          families after validation/deduplication. Invalidate retains data and marks stale without
          starting work. Clear removes data and metadata, fences generations, interrupts work, and
          publishes atomically. No zero-argument, wildcard, whole-runtime, or ordinary cache clear
          exists; complete removal belongs to runtime.dispose(). Only mapped events or explicit
          authoritative writes make emissions durable.

        - Resource, canonical-key, family-signature, state-union, transaction, finite-action, and
          stream examples appear below. Type examples appear under @plan.Types.O2; Cause handling
          also belongs to @plan.DiagnosticsV2.O2. CONTRACT.md §3 examples remain proposed and
          unaccepted. They do not supersede normative requirements. Identity and canonical-key
          ownership belong to @plan.RuntimeV2.O1 and @plan.RuntimeV2.O2.


        Example:
        ```ts
        type OrderInput = {
          orderId: string;
        };

        const orderById = resource({
          id: "everclear.order-by-id",
          key: ({ orderId }: OrderInput) => [orderId],
          lookup: ({ orderId }: OrderInput, { signal }) =>
            Effect.gen(function* () {
              const client = yield* OrderClient;
              return yield* client.getOrder(orderId, { signal });
            }),
          persist: true,
          staleTime: "30 seconds",
          gcTime: "5 minutes",
        });
        ```
        Example:
        ```text
        KBytes ::= "[" [ Value *( "," Value ) ] "]"
        Value ::= "null" | "true" | "false" | Number | String
                | "[" [ Value *( "," Value ) ] "]"
                | "{" [ Member *( "," Member ) ] "}"
        Member ::= String ":" Value
