    O4  Module/App universe, actors, Runtime, and React
        - module({ id, machines }) retains an exact keyed machine record. app({ id,
          persistenceVersion, modules }) flattens ordered, unaliased modules into exact App.M.
          Duplicate property names, machine values, or tooling ownership reject.
          Module IDs are unique tooling identity for CLI, trace, inspection, artifacts, and diffs.
          Renaming a Module ID breaks artifacts without an alias.
          Runtime and persistence identity stays compatible; history requires explicit migration.
          Every local/shared/Story-local actor uses a listed
          machine, every listed machine contributes graph and requirements to AppPlan, and
          compilation creates no actor. There are no dynamicMachines, automatic roots, runtime
          family lookup, XState setup, or machine.provide layer. Definitions shape, machines provide
          behavior, apps determine reachability, Implementations supply services, fixtures support
          tests. A running runtime cannot expand AppPlan.

        - The actor API and complete lifecycle are future targets. The temporary
          RuntimeSetup/Runtime exposes only app, construct(), and ready(). Inert
          actorRef(machine,id,{persist?:boolean}) has exactly machine, id, persist; persist defaults
          false and is metadata, not identity. It contains no input, bindings, ownership, callbacks,
          subscriptions, or disposal. Future methods are:
          - ensureActor returns { actor, dispose } and restores or creates.
          - getActor is lookup-only and grants no lease.
          - createActor makes a fresh local opaque ref with no stable ID and returns a lease.
          Concurrent ensures join construction.
          Dropping a lease does not dispose.
          Stable disposal tombstones the current runtime ref.
          Reuse is possible in a later runtime.
          Wire identity is `actor:` followed by length-prefixed UTF-8 machine-ID and
          stable-ID segments, as defined by @plan.RuntimeV2.O1 and @plan.RuntimeV2.O2. Context slots
          bind one-for-one to exact provider refs; missing, ambiguous, foreign, or cyclic
          bindings reject, and edges add no parentage, ownership, or command channel.
          Rebinding does not retain memory/context history.
          Readiness validates providers, codec, and AppPlan.
          It restores declarations and persistable actor-owned state.
          It caches success or terminal failure.
          Transaction and stream state restores only when its declaration and stable owner are
          both persistable.
          getActor never creates/adopts or grants ownership.
          A stable tombstone rejects lookup/ensure in-runtime.

        - Current ready() is a temporary-shell boundary.
        - Providers are acquired sequentially and lazily once per shell.
        - Cache and reuse the shell's first terminal Exit exactly once.
        - The Exit is success, typed ImplementationError failure, defect, or interruption.
        - Do not retry; preserve Cause classes.
        - Its E remains ImplementationError; do not invent Diagnostic mapping.
        - Current shell has no persistence, actors, Effect bridge, lifecycle, or disposal.
          Full Runtime and React lifecycle rules below remain future targets, not implementation
          claims.

        - Future Runtime bridges and final process/framework/CLI adapters are the only Effect
          execution edges. Reusable services/orchestration return Effects with exact A, Diagnostic
          E, and R; they never call runPromise*, runSync*, or another runner.
          Adapt each foreign rejecting Promise once at a named boundary using
          Effect.tryPromise({ try, catch }). Map unknown once to an owned Diagnostic.
          Forward AbortSignal when supported. Effect.promise is not the
          public pattern. Lifecycle is prepared | active | suspended | disposed. React names are
          FlowProvider, useActor(machine, options?), useActorByRef(ref), and useView(actor,
          selector). Readiness validates providers, codec, and AppPlan, then caches success or
          terminal failure. Transaction and stream state restores only when both declaration and
          stable owner are persistable.
          Boot, decoders, hydration, and DehydrateBarrier stay private.
          Runtime excludes boot/decode/mutable hydration/dehydrate, runtime.actor, roots, and
          public ManagedRuntime.
          It also excludes mutable resource/orchestrator services, test registries, and acknowledged
          dispatch.

        - Prepared buffers; active admits/owns resources; suspended preserves continuity, rejects
          commands, and has no live attachments; disposed is terminal. React local actors are fresh;
          by-ref is existing lookup without owner; both are command-only. useView is the only
          ordinary reactive path. Render prepares final actor/ref/handle/snapshot/mailbox without
          registration/work. Commit attaches, baseline-installs, activates, and drains once. Cleanup
          suspends that same actor without shell swap, grace period, or terminal disposal.
          Imperative create is immediately attached and running.
          useView tracks exact O descriptor/key reads at a tear-free boundary.
          It reruns on matching actor/StoreFanout publication using O4 equality.
          It has no comparator, registered views, or per-actor React context. Lifecycle
          publishes snapshot before evidence; retains start/restore/dispose and adds suspend/resume,
          not prepare. Future FlowDisposeError has _tag FlowDisposeError, cause
          Cause.Cause<unknown>, scope actor | runtime; asynchronous lease/runtime disposal is
          idempotent, cached, and terminal; blocked disposal identifies dependencies.

        - The temporary shell remains current. Actor, React, persistence, bridge, lifecycle, and
          disposal details remain future targets. Module/App, actor-ref, and Runtime setup examples
          appear below. Type overloads and selector examples appear under @plan.Types.O4 and
          @plan.Types.O5. CONTRACT.md §§1-3 examples remain proposed and unaccepted.
          They do not alter the Runtime authority at @plan.RuntimeV2.O2, @plan.RuntimeV2.O3,
          @plan.RuntimeV2.O4, @plan.RuntimeV2.O5, and @plan.RuntimeV2.D1.


        Example:
        ```ts
        const CoreModule = module({ id: "core", machines: { router: routerMachine, auth: authMachine } });
        const TodoApp = app({
          id: "todo-app",
          persistenceVersion: "1",
          modules: [CoreModule, TodosModule],
        });
        TodoApp.M.router;
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        const ref = actorRef(editorMachine, "primary-editor", { persist: true });
        const sharedLease = runtime.ensureActor(ref, { input, contextBindings });
        const sharedActor = runtime.getActor(ref);
        const localLease = runtime.createActor(editorMachine, { input, contextBindings });
        await sharedLease.dispose();
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        const setup = runtimeSetup({
          app: IncidentApp,
          implementation: IncidentLive,
          persistence: persistence({ storage: webStorage(window.localStorage), scope: "user:42" }),
        });
        const runtime = setup.construct();
        ```
    O5  Stories, behavior gateway, fixtures, and models
        - Story constructors are non-callable namespace methods: story.app(runtimeSetup, options?),
          story.machine(machine, options?), story.actor(machine, options?). App Story options are
          fixtures, maxTurns, title, description, tags. Machine Story adds exact required input,
          selected initial context, fixtures and the same metadata. Actor recipe options are exact
          input/contextBindings. Void-input machines reject authored input; focused Stories reject
          boot, refs, extra actors, raw memory, initial state, and snapshots. App Story requires
          typed RuntimeSetup, not bare app/runtime. maxTurns defaults to 100. Plans are inert until
          run and contain no handles, loops, predicates, callbacks, assertions, or branches. Every
          run uses production bootstrap, fixtures, TestClock, restoration, ensure, AppPlan
          validation, graph sealing, activation, and cleanup. Machine Story uses a private
          one-machine AppPlan and the same production engine.

        - behavior requires an explicit app and non-empty Story record whose keys are external IDs.
          It synchronously validates/registers only, acquiring no Implementation, Runtime, actor,
          Story, sink, or cleanup. Reject empty or duplicate keys, mixed/missing app, ActorRecipe
          values, and incompatible app/machine. App Stories use supplied RuntimeSetup; machine
          Stories require the machine in supplied App.M. Brand/AppPlan/gateway internals stay
          private; CLI is its only public consumer. Direct run and CLI share production
          runtime/kernel/evidence/cleanup; gateway is not a runner. Discovery uses one supplied App
          identity and has no runtime side effects.

        - Both Story kinds support process, advance, advanceTo, advanceToNextTimer, checkpoint, and
          run.
        - App Story adds target+event send; Machine Story adds target-free send and setContext.
        - App targets an exact app-owned ActorRef or Story recipe, never a machine family.
        - App Stories do not inject context.
        - process drains ready production work until another command or future time is needed.
        - It does not advance time or invent results.
          Clock movement does not process. checkpoint reads evidence immediately; run is the sole
          execution boundary.
          Builder commands are persistent: each returns a new plan and leaves its prefix unchanged.
          Derived plans replay the prefix independently in fresh isolated production Runtimes.
          This is static plan branching, never a live runtime fork.
          Plans share no live state, handles, or runtime.
          Checkpoint names accumulate in the inferred literal-name union.
          Duplicates and widened string reject.
          Known keys index; unknown literals fail.
          run.checkpoints maps exactly those names.
          It preserves evidence shape, run.end, and failure behavior.
          A private JavaScript/CLI lookup checks own-key membership
          and rejects unknown names with existing FlowUsageError semantics; there is no public
          dynamic-string getter or checkpoint-specific error.

        - Runs never inject pending results; complete Implementations supply behavior. Capture uses
          exact closure, one StoreState revision, snapshots, pending work, TestClock, and accepted
          runtime evidence prefix. Closure includes the single machine actor or all app recipes plus
          exact binding, target, and transitive-provider refs; unrelated actors are excluded.
          Capture performs no lookup/progress/work/time/create/dispose/restore. run.end follows
          commands before cleanup and does not mean completion. Failure preserves checkpoints and
          FlowStoryExecutionError with Cause, boundary, cleanup/cancellation/evidence diagnostics.
          Close admission before non-abortable finalization; clean in reverse
          dependency/deterministic phase order. Cleanup failure retains end evidence but never
          success; pre-capture failure never fabricates run.end.

        - fixture({ id, implementation, seeds? }) supplies complete services; seeds preload
          Runtime-owned resource state. Fixture provider overrides App provider by service identity;
          duplicate providers reject. Fixture Implementations replace complete service functions,
          preserving input, success, failure, cancellation, lifetime; kernels remain live. Seeds do
          not satisfy requirements, mock services, create global state, or authorize cache mutation.
          Providers construct once per Runtime; Story runs get fresh Runtime-scoped provider state
          unless app ownership explicitly shares it.

        - model(baseStory,{stateKey}) accepts only a command-empty, fresh machine Story. stateKey
          receives exact predicted snapshot and returns CanonicalKeyInput. Candidate events belong
          to each traversal call, not model/base state. Expose getShortestPaths and getSimplePaths
          only. Returned paths use ordinary Story result shape. Model does not run Effects,
          synthesize async routes, or expose replay/provide/clock helpers or named path/result
          classes. FlowStoryExecutionError is the sole named testing runtime class; its
          package-owned envelope retains the complete Cause accepted by @plan.Verification.V4.

        - Reject callable Story, .with, live runtime/bare app input, and focused overrides.
        - Reject extra actors, result injection, and implicit time processing.
        - Reject old flush/settle/perform/deliver/receive/setTime/replay/final helpers.
          Reject machine-family targets, live runtime forks, resumable checkpoints, and shared live
          state/handles/runtimes.
          Model rejects app/non-empty plans, cross-call candidate retention, function-mocking
          registries, parallel testing/path/result hierarchies, and Effects.

        - Constructor, gateway, command, checkpoint-evidence, fixture, and model examples appear
          below. Type-level options, checkpoint accumulation, recipe, fixture, and model examples
          appear under @plan.Types.O6. Runtime identity and ownership belong to @plan.RuntimeV2.O3 and
          @plan.RuntimeV2.O5. Diagnostic ownership belongs to @plan.DiagnosticsV2.O3; current root
          deferral is settled here. Future compatibility interpretation belongs to
          @plan.DiagnosticsV2.Q1.


        Example:
        ```ts
        story.app(runtimeSetup, options?);
        story.machine(machine, options?);
        story.actor(machine, options?);
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        const gateway = behavior({
          app: IncidentApp,
          stories: { smoke: incidentStory, "machine-model": incidentMachineStory },
        });
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        // Both Story kinds
        process();
        advance(duration);
        advanceTo(epochMilliseconds);
        advanceToNextTimer();
        checkpoint(name);
        run({ signal? });

        // App Story
        send(target, event);

        // Machine Story
        send(event);
        setContext(context);
        ```
        Example:
        ```ts
        const common = story
          .machine(editorMachine, options)
          .send(Editor.E.Opened())
          .process()
          .checkpoint("opened");

        const saved = common.send(Editor.E.SaveRequested()).process().checkpoint("saved");
        const discarded = common.send(Editor.E.Discarded()).checkpoint("discarded");
        const cancelled = common.send(Editor.E.Cancelled()).checkpoint("cancelled");

        const savedRun = await saved.run();
        const discardedRun = await discarded.run();
        savedRun.checkpoints.opened; // valid
        savedRun.checkpoints.saved; // valid
        savedRun.checkpoints.typo; // TypeScript error
        ```
        Example:
